## V. The Hard Problem of Consciousness (Foundational Claim)

Nagel, T. (1974). What Is It Like to Be a Bat? *The Philosophical Review*, 83(4), 435–450.
DOI: 10.2307/2183914

Chalmers, D. J. (1996). *The Conscious Mind: In Search of a Fundamental Theory*.
Oxford University Press. ISBN: 9780195117899

**Claim supported:** This architecture is built on the foundational claim that the hard
problem of consciousness — why and how physical processes give rise to subjective experience
— is real and unsolved. Nagel (1974) established the epistemic gap: what it is like to be
something cannot be known from the outside. Chalmers (1996) formally distinguished the hard
problem from tractable functional questions. A system designed to have something it is like
to be it cannot rest on eliminativist foundations. These citations ground the paper's
explicit position against functionalist reduction of consciousness.

