## VI. Anti-Confabulation Engine

Farquhar, S., Kossen, J., Kuhn, L., & Gal, Y. (2024). Detecting hallucinations in large
language models using semantic entropy. *Nature*, 630(8017), 625–630.
DOI: 10.1038/s41586-024-07421-0

Sui, P., Duede, E., Wu, S., & So, R. J. (2024). Confabulation: The Surprising Value of
Large Language Model Hallucinations. arXiv:2406.04175 [preprint]

**Claim supported:** Current LLMs conflate generation and verification in a single forward
pass — the mechanism that produces confabulation. Farquhar et al. (2024, Nature) provide
peer-reviewed methodology for detecting confabulation via semantic entropy, demonstrating
the measurability of the problem this layer addresses. Sui et al. (2024) establish the
distinction between confabulation (generation of plausible but ungrounded content believed
by the system) and hallucination (perceptual error), supporting the terminological precision
of this paper's claims. The anti-confabulation engine structurally separates generation
(thought-slaved layer) from verification (memory-slaved layer), precluding the conflation
that produces confabulation.

