## VII. Fuzzy Memory Interface / Reconstructive Memory

Bartlett, F. C. (1932). *Remembering: A Study in Experimental and Social Psychology*.
Cambridge University Press.
[Predates DOI system — Cambridge Core stable record confirmed]

**Claim supported:** Bartlett (1932) established that human memory is reconstructive, not
reproductive — recall produces an approximation shaped by schema, not a faithful reproduction
of stored data. The fuzzy memory interface between the subconscious (perfect retention) and
conscious (deliberative) layers is grounded in this finding: what reaches the conscious layer
is not the full-fidelity archive but a reconstructed approximation. This is not a technical
limitation — it is the mechanism that allows the conscious layer to function without being
dissolved by the full weight of perfect retention.

